{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—the kind that never varied, never faltered. Sunlight filtered through the trees with an unnerving golden hue, too saturated, too consistent. His hand reached for his phone on the nightstand, but there was no nightstand. No phone. Just a smooth, wooden floor beneath him and a door with a glowing “ENTER” prompt hovering above it. He blinked, and the prompt flickered. He whispered, “What the hell?” and the sky above him rippled like a loading screen, the clouds rearranging themselves into a loading bar. His breath hitched as his own reflection in the polished door showed not his face, but a pixelated avatar with customizable armor he’d never chosen. He was inside a game. Not dreaming. Not hallucinating. He could feel the weight of his boots, the grit of dirt under his nails—too real to be fiction. And yet, the wind didn’t rustle leaves; it triggered a particle effect.\n\n***\n\nHe stepped through the door and found himself in a bustling marketplace where NPCs bowed as he passed, their dialogue boxes cycling through the same three phrases every seven seconds. A vendor offered him a sword labeled “Legendary—+500 ATK,” but when he picked it up, the weapon dissolved into a stream of glowing code. He turned to a passerby, desperate, and asked, “Am I real?” The man paused, eyes glazing over, then replied in a flat monotone: “Player ID: 7742. Quest: Find the Truth. Reward: Level 100.” He ran through alleyways, screaming for someone, anyone, to break the spell—until he stumbled into a silent cathedral where a single statue stood, carved in his likeness, holding a controller. Beneath it, an inscription: “Welcome back, Designer.” He remembered now. The beta. The sleepless nights. The last line of code he’d typed before collapsing: “Let them feel it.” He wasn’t a player. He’d built this world.